WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.780
I'm going to tell you about a new video that
Reason just made.

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:06.460
It's about how to make your own handgun using
some very simple tools

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.440
and parts you can buy online.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.660
We're not even going to think about posting
it on YouTube.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.920
So a couple of months ago, after the march
for our lives rally,

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:19.259
YouTube said that they were no longer going
to allow users to post videos

00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:22.289
that contain instructions on manufacturing
a firearm.

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:25.479
The video and it's accompanying article are
part of a package of stories

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.660
in Reason's Burn After Reading issue.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.960
This includes a bunch of how-tos including
how to bake pot brownies, how to launder Bitcoin,

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.220
how to pick the lock on handcuffs and how
to hire an escort.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.719
The whole issue is a celebration of free speech

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:44.000
and our way of documenting how utterly futile
all kinds of prohibitions can be.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.769
The reason we made a video showing how easy
it is to DIY a Glock

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:50.680
is because we wanted to show how the First
Amendment

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.440
reinforces and backs up the Second Amendment.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.160
Basically if a bunch of journalists can build
a handgun in their kitchen

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.480
we can certainly assume that it will be too
hard

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.040
to keep guns out of the hands of motivated criminals.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.340
The question is, once we've made the video,

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.620
if YouTube prevents us from uploading it,
have they violated our First Amendment rights?

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.500
YouTube of old days was an amazing thing

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.160
that's become the digital library of Alexandria
on the internet.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:20.180
Karl Kasarda is the host of InRangeTV, which
is a weekly YouTube show about guns.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.880
They used to survive on ad revenue until YouTube
started demonetizing content.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.900
Hi, I'm Dennis Prager and welcome to my home.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:34.460
And last October, the conservative video production
shop Prager University sued YouTube

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.080
saying that they had restricted the audience
for their content

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.540
and alleging that the company was

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.920
"unlawfully censoring its educational videos

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.900
and discriminating against its right to freedom
of speech"

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.219
Here's the thing, YouTube is a private platform

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.380
and there's nothing in the First Amendment
or the Second

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.660
that requires them to run our gun video.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.300
Reason can turn down articles for any cause
that we choose.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.300
We can do it because we don't like the color
of the author's hair,

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.840
we don't like the font that she used in her
pitch email,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.780
we do not violate a single constitutional
amendment when we do that.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:09.180
Don't get me wrong I wish that YouTube would
run our video, it's awesome,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.480
but equally awesome is their right and ours
not to run content we don't like.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.140
Right now there are people in YouTube and
in Facebook

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.140
and other parts of this oligarchy of corporate
control over the internet

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.800
that have decided for whatever reason to use
this concept of community standards

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.740
to set fire to books in the library of Alexandria.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.460
Karl Kasarda is right that YouTube is the
closest thing that we have

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.220
to the library of Alexandria,

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.000
still doesn't mean that they have to run our
video.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.960
Wether you like PornHub or think pornography
is a blight I get that-

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.400
Once YouTube made it impossible for Kasarda
to make money on their platform

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.580
he just started posting his content to other
platforms, including PornHub.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.780
-psychological perspective, versus, yes, there
might be issues with pornography

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.320
depending on your belief system.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.000
Access to the megaphone of your choice is
not a right protected by the Constitution

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.560
and YouTube is hardly the first to test this.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.300
In 1972 a teacher's union president who was
running for state legislature

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.000
actually sued the Miami Herald

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.740
insisting that they run an editorial that
he had written after they attacked him in their pages.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:10.220
The Supreme Court correctly ruled that ordering
a newspaper to run an editorial

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.240
is a violation of the First Amendment.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.580
After all 'a newspaper is more than a passive
receptacle.'

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:19.580
Are you convinced after going through this
long process and digging into the suit

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.480
that Google's motives are idealogical?

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.340
There is no other possibility-

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.480
Prager University argued that YouTube

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.240
isn't entitled to the same editorial discretion as the Miami Herald

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.590
because it advertises itself as a platform
for free expression

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:37.440
that's committed to fostering a community
where everyone's voice can be heard.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.120
A federal judge, thankfully, dismissed the
Prager lawsuit

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:45.120
rejecting the company's argument that YouTube
is comparable to a government entity

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.910
and thus must be open access.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:50.600
A slew of other judges have arrived at the
same conclusion.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.040
YouTube deserves exactly the same latitude

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.180
that those judges gave to the Miami Herald in the 1970s

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.840
and that Reason enjoys today.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.400
And that's one of the things that our gun
video is celebrating.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.700
If YouTube doesn't want us to post it to their
site, their loss.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.500
We'll just post it to another platform.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.680
That's what the free and open internet is
about.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.180
So if you want to see our video, you can head
over to Reason.com or PornHub

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.100
and see how to make your very own unregistered
firearm.

